# Refract — Screens, menus, and audio

This file defines the screens Refract opens on and plays on, how a player moves
between them, and audio. The rules those screens present are in
`specs/beams.md`, the board's geometry and its node forms are in
`specs/board.md`, and the pointer and keyboard input the screens read are in
`specs/controls.md`. Every figure and every piece of screen copy below carries
the name this specification gives it.

## Presentation

Refract fixes no palette, no font, no node artwork, no beam rendering, and no
background. The look of the optical bench is yours. `specs/overview.md` states
the visual design, and `specs/board.md` states the few things about a node and a
beam a player has to read at a glance. One requirement is this file's own: every
piece of text a screen shows is legible against whatever sits behind it at the
logical stage size `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`).

## The two modes

Refract ships two ways to play, and the player picks one from the title menu.

| Mode | `state.mode` | What it is | Defined in |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Campaign | `campaign` | A fixed course of hand-built boards, worked through in order, with the board layouts in `specs/campaign-boards.md`. | `specs/modes/campaign.md` |
| Cascade | `cascade` | One unbroken sequence of generated boards that climbs in difficulty for as long as the player keeps solving. | `specs/modes/cascade.md` |

`state.mode` holds the mode the player chose and is read wherever the two differ.
Both modes are built, and both are reachable from the title menu of every build.

## Screens

`state.screen` names the screen the game is in. This file defines `title`,
`howto`, and `playing`, which both modes share. Each mode adds screens of its
own, and its file defines them, where its boards come from, and where each of its
screens leads: `specs/modes/campaign.md` for Campaign and
`specs/modes/cascade.md` for Cascade.

### `title`

The main menu, and where the game opens. It is the only place a mode is chosen.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `TITLE_TEXT` | `REFRACT` |
| Tagline | `TAGLINE_TEXT` | `DRAW THE LIGHT` |
| Menu | `TITLE_ITEMS` | `CAMPAIGN`, `CASCADE`, `HOW TO PLAY`, in that order |

The menu's items are stacked one above the next under the title and tagline. The
item at `state.menuIndex` is highlighted and drawn **distinctly** from the
others, so a player always sees which item `confirm` would accept. `menuIndex`
is `0` on arriving at the title. `up` and `down` move the highlight by one item
and wrap at both ends. `back` does nothing.

`confirm` takes the highlighted item:

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `CAMPAIGN` | Sets `state.mode = campaign` and starts the campaign, as `specs/modes/campaign.md` describes. |
| `CASCADE` | Sets `state.mode = cascade` and starts the sequence, as `specs/modes/cascade.md` describes. |
| `HOW TO PLAY` | Sets `screen = howto` and `menuIndex = 0`. |

Choosing a mode starts it fresh, whatever was played before it, so a player who
returns to the title and picks the other mode is never handed a board or a
progress figure left over from the first.

The bench may show behind the menu, dimmed or otherwise quieted, if that suits
the look you design.

### `howto`

How to play, written in a player's words rather than as rules of a system. The
rules are the same in both modes, so one screen serves both. It covers:

- what emitters, lenses, and crystals are, and that each channel has its own two
  emitters;
- that a beam is drawn by pressing on a node and dragging along the nodes it
  should thread, and that releasing leaves the beam as drawn;
- that a beam runs between its channel's two emitters and threads every lens of
  that channel;
- that a segment joins a node to any of the eight nodes around it, diagonals
  included, and never reaches across an empty cell;
- that a beam never touches another channel's emitters or lenses;
- that any beam may cross a crystal, and that every one of a crystal's charges
  has to be spent, no more and no fewer;
- that a segment is drawn once, and no two beams share one;
- that two segments never cross, so only one of the two diagonals of a square of
  four cells can be drawn;
- that a move the rules do not allow simply does not take, and the beam stays as
  it was;
- the controls, naming the key bound to the `clear` action.

`back` returns to `title` with `menuIndex = 0`.

### `playing`

The live board, and the only screen a beam is drawn on. It is reached in both
modes and works the same way in each. It shows:

| Element | What the player sees |
| --- | --- |
| The board | Every cell of the current board, each node drawn at its cell center. |
| The beams | Every segment drawn so far, in its channel's hue, visibly connecting the centers of the cells it links. |
| Per-channel progress | For each channel present on the board, whether that channel's beam is complete, carried on that channel's own hue and silhouette so a player reads at a glance which channels are still open. |
| The clear control | The key bound to the `clear` action, named on screen. |

A crystal shows its charge count and how many of those charges are currently
spent, as `specs/board.md` describes, so a player tracks a crystal without
counting segments.

The current mode adds its own readouts to this screen, and states them itself:
`specs/modes/campaign.md` for Campaign and `specs/modes/cascade.md` for Cascade.
Those sit clear of the board, whose extent is given in `specs/board.md`.

## What advances on each screen

`state.simTime` accumulates the frame's delta time on every update, whatever the
screen, and input is read on every screen. Beyond that:

| Screen | What advances |
| --- | --- |
| `title`, `howto` | Nothing. |
| `playing` | The live trace, the beams it edits, and whether the board is solved. |

Nothing advances on the screens the modes add.

## Menu navigation

Menus are keyboard only, through the registered actions in `specs/controls.md`:
`up` and `down` move the highlight, `confirm` accepts the highlighted item, and
`back` returns. The pointer draws beams and does not operate menus. `mute` is
read on every screen.

## Audio

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The audio layer is part of the runtime you write. Define and play exactly the
five cues in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus. Define and play exactly the five cues
in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus: define exactly the five cues in
`CUES` from the game instance's `initialize` and play them from the world's
audio inside a tick, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{/if}}

| Cue | Constant | Played when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `connect` | `CUES.connect` | A segment is added. |
| `retract` | `CUES.retract` | A segment is removed. |
| `channel-complete` | `CUES.channelComplete` | A channel's beam becomes complete. |
| `solved` | `CUES.solved` | The board becomes solved. |
| `clear` | `CUES.clear` | Every beam is cleared. |

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Each is a distinct short sound, so the five events are told apart by ear. Each
is played on the frame its event happens, from `update`, and at most once on
that frame; a frame that raises more than one of them plays each of those once.
`clear` plays only when there was a segment to remove. The five cues are the
same in both modes.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Each is a distinct short sound, so the five events are told apart by ear. Each
is played on the frame its event happens, from `update`, and at most once on
that frame; a frame that raises more than one of them plays each of those once.
`clear` plays only when there was a segment to remove. The five cues are the
same in both modes.
{{else}}
Each is a distinct short sound, so the five events are told apart by ear. Each
is played on the frame its event happens, by the code that raised it, and at
most once on that frame; a frame that raises more than one of them plays each
of those once. `clear` plays only when there was a segment to remove. The five
cues are the same in both modes.
{{/if}}

Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the runtime. The game binds
the `mute` action to the runtime's mute bit and toggles it from any screen, then
mirrors that bit into `state.muted` every frame. The game stays fully playable
with sound muted.

## Out of scope

- Network or online play.
- Touch and gamepad input. The board is drawn with the pointer, and the menus
  are driven from the keyboard.
- Persistence of progress or settings between sessions. Each session starts
  fresh.
